    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: "Texas Hold'em",
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Texas Hold'em is the world's most popular poker variant. Each player receives two private hole cards, and five community cards are dealt face-up on the table. Players make the best five-card poker hand using any combination of their hole cards and the community cards. The game features four rounds of betting and combines skill, strategy, and psychology.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Robstown, Texas, USA\nTime Period: Early 1900s (circa 1920s)\nCreator(s): Unknown (developed in Texas card rooms)\nEtymology: Named after Texas, where it originated, and "Hold\'em" refers to players holding their own cards while sharing community cards',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2 (heads-up)\nMaximum Players: 10 (full ring)\nOptimal Players: 6-9\nPartnerships: No (individual play)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nPoker chips for betting\nDealer button\nSmall blind and big blind markers\nTable (preferably oval for 6-10 players)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: 2 (hole cards, face down)\nCommunity Cards: 5 (dealt face up in stages)\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise, starting with small blind\n--',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: "Pineapple: Each player is dealt three hole cards and must discard one before the first round of betting. Otherwise same as Texas Hold'em.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Crazy Pineapple: Each player is dealt three hole cards and keeps all three until after the second betting round. Each active player must discard one card immediately before the turn is dealt.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Crazy Pineapple Hi-Lo 8 or Better: Crazy Pineapple with pot split between highest and lowest hands. Low hand: five different ranks 8 or lower, aces low, no straights or flushes. Best low is A-2-3-4-5.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Tahoe (Wichita Hold'em): Each player is dealt three hole cards and keeps all three until showdown. A player may use 0, 1 or 2 hole cards (not all three) with board cards to make their final hand.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Irish: Each player is dealt four hole cards, kept until after the second betting round. Before the turn, each active player must discard two cards, keeping two for turn and river.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Informal (No Burn): Home game variant: community cards dealt face down to the table at the start; three turned up after first betting round, one after second, one after third. No burn cards.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'No Limit: Players may bet any amount up to their full stack at any time. Most common in tournaments and cash games.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Fixed Limit: Bet and raise amounts are fixed (e.g. small bet preflop and flop, big bet turn and river). Typically one bet plus three or four raises per round.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Pot Limit: Maximum bet or raise is the current pot size. Balances no-limit and fixed-limit play.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Heads-Up (2 Players): Two players only. Dealer posts small blind, non-dealer posts big blind. Action order and strategy differ from full table.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Short-Handed (3-5 Players): Played with three to five players. Tighter hand requirements than full ring; position and aggression matter more.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Full Ring (6-10 Players): Standard table size. Optimal with six to nine players. Tighter play than short-handed.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Straddle: Optional third blind (typically double the big blind) posted by the player to the left of the big blind before cards are dealt. Creates a larger pot and different action order.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Casino Versions: Formal card-room and casino rules: mandatory burn before each community deal, strict betting order, and house procedures.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
